{"slides":[{"k":"title","h":"HOW do we close it well?","sub":"Session 4 · Live class · Negotiation, contract and handover","note":"Welcome everyone to the last session and ask for cameras on: today is the most interactive day of the course, with a full negotiation role-play. Remind them the video lessons covered the theory (the course deck), so we will not repeat it; we will decide, argue and practise. Say the one-line goal: by 13:00 every Sourcing Case has a negotiation one-pager, an award logic, a regulatory clause checklist and a 90-day handover plan, which completes the file for the capstone. Recall the big idea one last time: S2C is a decision chain, not a document chain, and today we close its last three links, where value is most often lost. Two minutes."},{"k":"table","h":"Today's plan","t":[["Time","Block","How"],["09:00","Opening and Fieldwork 3 debrief","Chat, poll, trios"],["09:15","4.1 Negotiation planning","Dilemma, your one-pager"],["09:50","4.2 Auction or negotiate?","Dilemma, six-scenario quick-fire"],["10:15","Case 4: the Kelvar renewal","Role-play in breakout rooms of four"],["10:55","Kelvar debrief","Poll, reveal"],["11:10","Break",""],["11:20","4.3 Award and contract","Cleaning award, the losing bidder"],["11:50","4.4 Contracts in a regulated insurer","DORA debate, Northbyte exit gap"],["12:25","Break",""],["12:35","4.5 Handover: contract to value","Your first 90 days"],["12:50","Capstone briefing and close",""]],"note":"Walk the clock in one minute and point out the two breaks. Flag the peak: the Kelvar role-play from 10:15, which needs everyone on time because rooms are built in fours. The times are a few minutes shifted from the agenda in the session overview so the role-play gets its full 35 minutes; tell them so if anyone compares. Ask everyone to keep the workbook open on Practice 4.1 to 4.5 and Case 4. If you start late, take the time from the 4.2 quick-fire, never from the role-play."},{"k":"discuss","h":"Warm-up: the deal you regret","q":"Type one thing you gave away in a past negotiation that you would never give again.","b":["One line, no supplier names","Wait for my signal, then send all at once","Then: was it the price, or something else?"],"min":4,"format":"Chat waterfall","note":"Ask everyone to type but hold Enter until you say go, so answers land together and nobody copies. Read six or seven aloud and cluster them live: price concessions versus everything else (term, auto-renewal, indexation without a cap, unlimited liability caps for the supplier, no exit support, payment terms). Expected pattern: most regrets are not the headline price but a term that kept costing money for years, such as the 9% Kelvar-style uplift or a silent roll-over. Say: that is why today is about the whole deal, not the number on the first page. Keep it light and fast; four minutes."},{"k":"poll","poll":"s4-l1","note":"Launch the poll and give 30 seconds. Use the result to tell the room how to listen today: those who answered sole-source should pay most attention to the Kelvar role-play; those with RFP and BAFO to the cleaning award in 4.3; those with an auction to the dilemma in 4.2 and the preconditions. If many people answer 'RFQ, then an e-auction', ask one of them aloud whether their category passed the preconditions check from Session 3; if it did not, that is a flag to resolve before the capstone. Two minutes."},{"k":"discuss","h":"Fieldwork 3 debrief: did the weights survive?","q":"You took your evaluation model to the business owner before issue. Did it get better or worse?","b":["What did they change, and why?","Did anyone push price above 50% for a service? What did you say?","Did you attach the draft contract to the pack?","One thing you would do differently next time"],"min":9,"format":"Trios in breakout rooms, then two voices in plenary","note":"Send trios to breakout rooms for six minutes, two minutes per person. Back in plenary, take two voices only. Push on two points. First: weights are fixed before issue; if anyone changed them after seeing bids, name it calmly as an equal-treatment and governance failure, not a detail. Second: the draft contract in the pack; those who attached it will negotiate far less after award, which links straight to today. A common answer is 'the business wanted price at 70%' for cleaning or security; ask how that squares with wage floors and abnormally low bids. Do not solve every case here; note open issues for the capstone. Nine minutes in total."},{"k":"section","n":"4.1","h":"Negotiation planning","sub":"Most of the outcome is fixed before anyone enters the room","note":"Short transition, 60 seconds, as recap: they know BATNA, ZOPA, the four numbers per issue (opening, target, walk-away, priority) and conditional concessions from the video lessons. Say it in one sentence: power and preparation decide most of a negotiation; talent in the room decides the rest. Ask for a show of hands: who has ever walked into a negotiation without a written walk-away? Most hands go up. Smile and move on."},{"k":"discuss","h":"Dilemma: concede on term or on price?","q":"Bidder D offers the best quality but insists on four years. Bidder B is cheaper on three. What do you trade?","b":["What interest could sit behind \"four years\"?","Is a longer term cheap or expensive for Nordvia?","Which option structure gives D what it needs and keeps our control?","What do you ask for in exchange?"],"min":10,"format":"Vote in the chat (term / price / neither), then open floor","note":"Ask everyone to type TERM, PRICE or NEITHER in the chat and send together. Call one voice from each camp. Then ask the key question: what makes four years important to D? Steer to the interest, not the position: D plans robot floor care and must pay back the investment (this is the pre-work quiz example). Once the interest is visible, the answer is not a straight concession: offer a structure such as 3+1+1 with options tied to KPIs, so D sees a path to five years while Nordvia keeps the exit if quality drops; in exchange ask for price movement and KPI-based service credits. Pitfall to name: conceding term unconditionally because it 'costs nothing'; a long term without performance options is expensive when quality slips. This sets up the BAFO in 4.3, where D accepts 3+1+1. Ten minutes; stop when arguments repeat."},{"k":"exercise","h":"Your negotiation one-pager","min":15,"where":"Individually, then 3 minutes in pairs · Practice 4.1","b":["Power position: Kraljic and how the supplier sees you","Our interests, their likely interests","BATNA: how you strengthen yours, your estimate of theirs","At least four issues with opening, target and walk-away","Three concessions: cost to us, only in exchange for what","Roles: lead, analyst, business owner, sponsor"],"note":"Give 12 minutes of individual work on their own Sourcing Case, then 3 minutes in pairs: the partner reads the one-pager and asks one hard question, ideally 'what do you do if they say no?'. Walk the room virtually by asking two people to share screens briefly. Watch for three common gaps: a walk-away that is the same as the target, a single issue (price only) so nothing can be traded, and a BATNA written as 'go to tender' when no tender is possible in time. Those working on a panel call-off or an RFQ can plan the BAFO or the negotiation with the top-ranked bidder. Remind them this page is also the negotiation slide of the capstone."},{"k":"discuss","h":"Your walk-away: a number or a hope?","q":"\"If you have not improved your BATNA, your walk-away is a bluff.\"","b":["What did you write as your BATNA? Could you start it tomorrow?","What would it take to make it real: an RFI, a second supplier, re-specifying?","Who on our side could undermine your walk-away?"],"min":8,"format":"Agree / disagree, then open floor","note":"Ask for thumbs up or down and take one voice from each side. The debrief point of Practice 4.1: a BATNA is built by work, not assumed; an RFI to alternatives, a qualified second supplier, re-specification or insourcing make it real. Expect someone to say that in sole-source cases there is no BATNA; answer with the Kelvar logic you are about to play: the BATNA may be weak for this cycle and real for the next, and an honest partial BATNA (a bridge on fewer users plus an RFI) still moves the seller. Bring in the third prompt: the business owner or an IT colleague talking to the supplier in parallel destroys the walk-away; one voice, aligned before the meeting (Walton and McKersie's intra-organisational bargaining). Eight minutes."},{"k":"section","n":"4.2","h":"Auction or negotiate?","sub":"Choose the negotiation type first, then the mechanism, then the Coupa set-up","note":"One-minute recap: an e-auction is a mechanism for one kind of negotiation, competitive leverage with a precise specification and enough bidders; it is not a negotiation strategy. Ask the room to recall the preconditions from Session 3 in the chat in one word each: specification, bidders, capacity, price decides, BATNA, relationship. Then go to the dilemma."},{"k":"discuss","h":"Dilemma: the director wants an auction","q":"B and D are tied on the HQ cleaning lot. Your director says: \"Put them in a live auction and let them fight it out.\" Do you?","b":["Which auction preconditions hold here, and which fail?","What happens to quality and wages in a price-only race?","What is the alternative that still creates pressure?","How do you say no to your director, with evidence?"],"min":12,"format":"Two camps, 3 minutes to prepare, then debate","note":"Split the room: odd breakout numbers argue for the auction, even against; 3 minutes to prepare, then one speaker per camp, 2 minutes each, then open floor. Expected arguments: two bidders only, labour-intensive service, quality already differs (74 versus 81), wage-floor risk already seen with Bidder C, and a five-year relationship to start. The evidence: auction literature describes typical reductions of 5 to 15 percent, but realised savings were lower than reported (Emiliani and Stec), open-bid formats hurt trust without higher savings (Jap), and complex or incompletely specified work with few bidders fits auctions poorly (Bajari, McMillan and Tadelis). Steer to the answer: a BAFO round on price and terms against the fixed evaluation model, with a should-cost floor. If the pro camp is strong, concede that an auction above a quality threshold is possible in principle for some quality-led services, but not with two bidders. Coach the last prompt: say no with the preconditions checklist, not with opinion. Twelve minutes."},{"k":"exercise","h":"Quick-fire: call the mode","min":12,"where":"Chat, one scenario at a time · Practice 4.2","b":["1 Office supplies catalogue: fixed basket, six qualified suppliers","2 HQ cleaning: four bids, two above the quality threshold","3 Senior developer for six months under the IT services panel","4 Kelvar core licence renewal","5 Northbyte claims IT managed service at end of term","6 Telecom lines for 40 sites in three countries, bundle discounts"],"note":"Read each scenario aloud, give 20 seconds, and ask everyone to send the mode in the chat on your signal; then reveal and spend a minute only where the room splits. Answers: 1 RFQ then English reverse auction, then a Coupa catalogue. 2 RFP, negotiation rounds and BAFO via a follow-on event. 3 Panel mini-competition from the call-off template, or a documented direct award if all terms are fixed. 4 Sole-source negotiation on data, with an RFI to alternatives, never an auction. 5 Multi-stage dialogue and BAFO, or a sole-source renegotiation if no exit is feasible in time; either way a tested exit plan is needed (4.4). 6 Sourcing Optimization with package bids and scenarios. Expect a split on 5 and on 3; that is good, it shows the type is a judgement. Say clearly: overtime, minimum decrement and sealed envelopes in Coupa are settings to confirm in the Group's tenant, not facts to promise. Finish with scenario 7 in the workbook: their own case."},{"k":"section","n":"Case 4","h":"The Kelvar renewal","sub":"A sole-source negotiation role-play · Nordvia Insurance (fictional)","note":"Say this is the centre of the day: a real-feeling sole-source negotiation where you cannot run a tender and an auction would be a bluff. Before showing the next slide, send the seller brief privately (Case tab, teacher-only section) to one participant per room; if a colleague from IT vendor management joined, use them as sellers. Check every seller has it before you open rooms. One minute."},{"k":"example","h":"Kelvar: the buyer's facts","tag":"Nordvia Insurance (fictional) · illustrative targets","p":"€11.2M a year, core insurance software, renewal June 2027. A protective notice was served in time. Kelvar opens at +9% (about €12.2M a year) for five years.","b":["Usage: an estimated 20–25% of named users unused","BATNA: no migration before 2027; the CIO approved an RFI to two alternatives and a 12–24-month bridge on fewer users","Targets: about €10.0M a year, uplift cap 3%, three years, right to reduce volume 10% a year, transition assistance","Walk-away: €11.2M flat for three years, with reduction rights and DORA clauses","Supports critical functions: DORA Art. 30(3) clauses are not negotiable","Check: do other Group entities license Kelvar?"],"note":"Walk the buyer facts in three minutes; the buyer brief is in Case 4 too. Stress three things. One: the protective notice changed the power balance; without it the contract would have auto-renewed again. Two: be honest about the BATNA; no migration is possible by June 2027, so the credible pressure is rightsizing, the RFI to alternatives and the next cycle, not a threat to leave tomorrow. Three: DORA clauses are a floor, not a trading chip; negotiate how they are met, never whether. All figures are illustrative. Do not mention the seller's year-end or SaaS interest; the buyers must discover them."},{"k":"exercise","h":"Role-play: negotiate the Kelvar renewal","min":35,"where":"Breakout rooms of four · Case 4 in the workbook","b":["Roles: lead buyer, analyst, Kelvar account manager, observer","10 min: buyers prepare the one-pager; the seller reads the private brief","20 min: negotiate","5 min: observer feedback: first offer, every concession, interests found","Record the outcome per issue against target and walk-away"],"note":"Brief in two minutes, then open rooms at about 10:20 and close at 10:55. Broadcast at minute 10 ('start negotiating'), at minute 28 ('two minutes to close') and at minute 30 ('observers now'). Visit rooms silently with camera off. If a room is stuck on price only, whisper to the seller to hint at 'timing on our side'; if buyers concede DORA audit rights, do not interrupt, use it in the debrief. Typical pitfalls to watch: buyers who open with the threat of migration nobody believes; buyers who never ask why Kelvar wants five years; analysts who do not track the money value of concessions. Rooms that do not close record where they stopped and why; no deal beyond walk-away is a valid outcome."},{"k":"poll","poll":"s4-l2","note":"Launch the poll the moment rooms close, before people relax, and show the spread. Do not reveal the seller's limits yet. Ask a room that landed below €10.3M how they did it, and a room above €11.2M or with no deal what blocked them. Expected: rooms that discovered the seller's interests landed close to the seller's floor; rooms that argued price only stayed near the opening. Two minutes."},{"k":"discuss","h":"Kelvar debrief: which question unlocked it?","q":"What did the seller really want, and which question made them say it?","b":["Sellers: what did the buyers ask that made you open up?","Who found the year-end deadline? The SaaS interest? The reference?","Did anyone trade away a DORA clause? What should have happened?","What would you do 18 months before the next renewal?"],"min":8,"format":"Sellers first, then open floor","note":"Ask two sellers to answer first: what made you share, and what kept you quiet? Then observers: which concession came first and was it conditional? Typical unlocking questions: 'what does a good outcome look like for you this year?', 'why five years?', 'what is your plan for this platform?'. Name the pattern: interests come out when buyers ask and listen, not when they repeat the target. On DORA: if a room conceded unrestricted audit, ask what they should have done; answer: keep the right, agree how it is met (certifications, pooled audits). For the last prompt, collect: usage audit early, RFI to alternatives running, Group volume checked, renewal decision date in the pipeline. Eight minutes."},{"k":"example","h":"The reveal: what a good outcome looked like","tag":"Illustrative · the seller's brief, revealed","p":"Kelvar's fiscal year ends in May and the deal is in the forecast. Kelvar wants customers on its SaaS edition, and an insurance reference in the region.","b":["Unused named users removed before any price discussion","About €10.3M a year for three years, uplift capped at 3%","Right to reduce volume each year","DORA clauses kept, met through certifications and pooled audits","In exchange: a joint SaaS evaluation in 2028 and a reference, signed before Kelvar's year end"],"note":"Now reveal the seller's brief. Walk the outcome in three minutes and say why it is good for both sides: Kelvar got timing, a SaaS path and a reference, which cost Nordvia little; Nordvia got rightsizing, a capped uplift, flexibility and the regulatory floor. This is integrative negotiation in a sole-source setting, creating value before claiming it (Lax and Sebenius). Point out that €10.3M is below the old €11.2M and far below the €12.2M opening, without any real threat to leave. Ask people to fill the outcome table in Case 4 tonight if they did not finish. Then send everyone to the break."},{"k":"statement","h":"Break: 10 minutes","p":"Back at 11:20. Open Practice 4.3: the HQ cleaning award.","note":"Keep this slide up with a visible timer. Thank the sellers for playing their part. Use the break to note two or three good questions you heard in the rooms; you will reuse them in the close. Remind people to return on time; 4.3 starts with a calculation."},{"k":"section","n":"4.3","h":"Award and contract","sub":"Leverage is highest before signature: what is not in the contract will not happen","note":"Welcome people back. One-minute recap: the award recommendation is one document approvers can sign without reopening the file; the contract structure (architecture, SLAs, price mechanism, term, exit, protection) is where the negotiated value is locked in. Remind them of the HQ cleaning case from Session 3: Bidder C excluded, B and D tied within 0.1 points, so a BAFO round was run."},{"k":"exercise","h":"Award the HQ cleaning lot","min":12,"where":"Pairs · Practice 4.3","b":["Bidder C excluded: staffing hours failed the wage-floor check","BAFO (illustrative): B €600K, quality 74; D €632K, quality 81, now on 3+1+1","Weights 50/50; price score = lowest price ÷ bid × 100","Compute both totals, then test 60/40 quality/price","Write the rationale and the contract conditions"],"note":"Give 10 minutes in pairs and 2 minutes to compare in plenary. Expected numbers: B 74 × 0.5 + 100 × 0.5 = 87.0; D price score 600 ÷ 632 × 100 = 94.9, total 81 × 0.5 + 94.9 × 0.5 = 88.0. At 60/40: B 84.4, D 86.6. D wins under both weightings, so the recommendation is robust; the sensitivity test is what convinces approvers. Conditions the room should name: KPIs and service credits with a cap, options tied to KPI results, indexation on the labour share only with a named wage index and a cap, staff-transfer obligations, exit and transition assistance. Pitfall: recommending B because it is cheaper and forgetting that the evaluation model, fixed before issue, decides. All bids are fictional."},{"k":"discuss","h":"The losing bidders call","q":"Bidder C, excluded, calls you: \"You threw us out for being cheap.\" What do you tell them, and what do you tell Bidder B?","b":["Did we ask C to explain its price before excluding it?","What goes into a written debrief, and what never does?","Courtesy period before signature: why bother in the private sector?","How do debriefs pay off in the next tender?"],"min":10,"format":"Role-play in plenary: one volunteer calls, one answers","note":"Ask a volunteer to play Bidder C's sales director and another to answer as the buyer, two minutes, live; then open the floor. Key points to land: an abnormally low bid is clarified before exclusion, the bidder is asked to explain its price and hours (CCS guidance explicitly asks buyers to seek explanations), and the decision is documented; the answer to C is about compliance with the wage floor, not about being cheap. A written debrief gives the bidder its own scores, strengths and weaknesses, never another bidder's prices. A short courtesy period before signing is good practice privately, while public law imposes a standstill. The payoff: better bids next time and fewer disputes. Pitfall: debriefing by phone only, or saying 'it was close' without evidence. Ten minutes."},{"k":"section","n":"4.4","h":"Contracts in a regulated insurer","sub":"Negotiate how a clause is met, never whether","note":"One-minute recap: classify first (outsourcing? critical or important function? ICT service under DORA? personal data? staff transfer?), with the outsourcing and DORA owners; the classification is the insurer's own documented assessment. Solvency II Art. 49 keeps the insurer fully responsible; Art. 274 of Delegated Regulation 2015/35 defines the written agreement; DORA Art. 28 and Art. 30 set the pre-contract assessment, the register and the clauses. Say clearly: EIOPA's cloud outsourcing guidelines were withdrawn from 17 January 2025; do not quote them as current. And: this is orientation, not legal advice; Legal and Compliance confirm."},{"k":"poll","poll":"s4-l3","note":"Launch the poll and show results. Ask one person from the largest group what usually happens: who asks to weaken the clause, and who agrees? Expected answers: audit and access, and exit, are the clauses suppliers push back on most, and they are exactly the ones DORA Art. 30(3) requires for critical or important functions. If 'None' is large, ask gently how they know; often the clauses are in the template but softened in a side letter or a supplier paper. Three minutes."},{"k":"discuss","h":"Which DORA clause would you never drop?","q":"Kelvar says: \"We accept DORA, but no unlimited on-site audits.\" Which clause do you defend to the end, and how do you meet them halfway?","b":["Audit and access, exit, termination rights, subcontracting, data location: rank them","What is non-negotiable by law, and what is only the way it is met?","Certifications, pooled audits, notice for on-site visits: enough?","Who in the Group must agree before you accept a variant?"],"min":12,"format":"Pairs rank the five, then plenary","note":"Give pairs four minutes to rank the five clauses for an ICT service supporting a critical or important function, then collect the top choice of each pair in the chat. Expect a split between audit and access and exit; both are mandatory under Art. 30(3) for critical or important functions, so the right answer is that none can be dropped; the trap in the question is the word 'drop'. The negotiation is about how: certifications and pooled audits to meet audit rights, reasonable notice for on-site visits, a defined transition period for exit. For Solvency II critical outsourcing, the written agreement must also give the insurer, its auditor and the supervisor access to information and premises (Art. 274). Last prompt: variants go to Legal and the DORA or outsourcing owner, never agreed alone in the room. Twelve minutes."},{"k":"exercise","h":"Case 4, part 2: the Northbyte exit gap","min":15,"where":"Pairs, then plenary · Case 4 and Practice 4.4","b":["Claims IT managed service, €19.4M a year, contract to December 2027","Supports a critical function; in the DORA register; no tested exit plan","Find three gaps against DORA Art. 28(8) and 30(3)","One action and an owner for each gap, before end 2027","Does a renewal or change trigger a Solvency II notification? Who decides?","Then: start the regulatory checklist for your own case"],"note":"Eight minutes in pairs, then four minutes in plenary, then three minutes to start Practice 4.4 on their own case. Expected gaps: no documented and tested exit strategy (Art. 28(8)); alternatives and a transition plan not identified; contract likely missing Art. 30(3) exit and transition clauses and an adequate transition period; renewal timing that leaves no room to test an exit. Actions: name an exit plan owner now, desk-test the plan within months, identify alternatives through an RFI, add the clauses at renewal. On notification: if Northbyte is critical outsourcing under Solvency II, a renewal or material change may require notifying the supervisor; the outsourcing owner and Compliance decide, not Procurement. If a Compliance or DORA register colleague is present, give them the floor here."},{"k":"statement","h":"Break: 10 minutes","p":"Back at 12:35. Think of the biggest contract you manage: when is its renewal decided?","note":"Keep the timer on screen. The question on the slide primes the poll that opens 4.5. Use the break to check who has not yet booked a capstone date or a business-owner review; you will ask them before the end."},{"k":"section","n":"4.5","h":"Handover: from contract to value","sub":"A contract nobody buys against is a document, not a deal","note":"One-minute recap: mobilisation by category, the contract live in Coupa (CLM record, catalogue, punch-out or contract-backed buying, price lists, date alerts), governance where effort follows value and risk, savings agreed with Finance, and a renewal decision date in the pipeline. Remind them of the Nordvia lessons: Telmo rolled over twice, Kelvar auto-renewed at +9%. Never again."},{"k":"poll","poll":"s4-l4","note":"Launch the poll and show results. Expect a large share in 'less than 9 months', 'after the notice deadline' or 'I do not know'. Link it to the facts: most of the pipeline comes from expiring contracts, so renewal dates are the cheapest forecast Procurement has. The rule: set the decision date at least as early as a re-tender would need, which for complex categories is often a year or more before expiry, and put it in the sourcing pipeline with a Coupa CLM alert. Two minutes."},{"k":"discuss","h":"Whose saving is it?","q":"\"Procurement's job ends at signature. Delivery is the business owner's problem.\"","b":["Who owns the contract the day after signature?","If invoices do not match contract prices, who notices?","Who confirms the saving to Finance, and when was the method agreed?"],"min":5,"format":"Agree / disagree, fast","note":"Quick thumbs vote, then two voices. Most will disagree in principle but admit it is how things run. Land three points: a named contract owner from day one, invoices validated against Coupa contract prices, and savings counted with a baseline and method agreed with Finance before the event. Use the Group planning assumption carefully: about 6% per three-year cycle (2% savings plus 4% cost avoidance) is a planning assumption, not a benchmark or a promise. Five minutes, then straight into the practice."},{"k":"exercise","h":"Your first 90 days","min":8,"where":"Individually · Practice 4.5","b":["Contract owner after signature","Buying channel in Coupa: catalogue, punch-out, contract-backed, call-offs","Days 0–30, 31–60, 61–90: activity, owner, date","Three KPIs and how they are measured","Renewal decision date and notice deadline"],"note":"Give eight minutes; say they can finish it as part of Fieldwork 4. Ask two people to read their renewal decision date and notice deadline aloud: is the gap long enough for a re-tender? Watch for 'to confirm' as the buying channel; push them to name it, because a contract with no channel is where maverick buying starts. For ICT cases remind them that mobilisation includes the DORA register entry and an exit-plan owner."},{"k":"steps","h":"Capstone: My Sourcing Case","s":[["This week","Agree the one-pager and mandate with your business owner"],["Within 2 weeks","Regulatory checklist reviewed with Legal or Compliance"],["Within 3 weeks","Handover plan agreed with the future contract owner; renewal date in the pipeline"],["Before the capstone","Rehearse with your trio"],["In about 4 weeks","10 minutes plus 5 of questions to the panel"]],"note":"Brief the capstone in four minutes. Panel: the Head of Procurement, a business owner and a Risk or Compliance colleague; online, cameras on. Content: need and category, power position, strategy and route to market, event or negotiation design, negotiation plan or outcome, contract and regulatory checklist, handover and renewal date. Weights: category insight and power 20%, strategy and route to market 25%, event and negotiation design 20%, contract and regulation 20%, handover and value 15%. Repeat the two rules clearly: the tool follows the strategy, and expect 'why this route and not another?'; a case the business owner has not seen does not pass. Ask anyone without a booked date to book it before leaving today."},{"k":"takeaways","h":"Four sessions, one decision chain","b":["Analyse the category and the power position first","Choose the strategy, then the tool: tender, panel, negotiation or auction","Run fair, comparable events; auction only when the preconditions hold","Negotiate on interests, data and an honest BATNA","Contract for regulation and value, and hand over so the contract is used"],"note":"Close the course by walking the five lines, one sentence each, and connect each to a moment from today: the auction dilemma, the Kelvar questions that unlocked the seller, the DORA clause nobody may drop, the renewal dates. Read one or two good questions you heard in the role-play rooms. Then ask everyone to type in the chat one decision they will take differently on Monday, and read a few aloud. Thank the participants, any guests and sellers, and the Group sponsors; remind them of the capstone date and where to find the workbook and templates. End on time."}],"polls":[{"id":"s4-l1","q":"How will your Sourcing Case most likely be closed?","options":["RFP, negotiation rounds and BAFO","RFQ, then an e-auction","Panel mini-competition or documented direct award","Sole-source negotiation with the incumbent","Multi-stage dialogue and BAFO"]},{"id":"s4-l2","q":"Kelvar: where did the annual price land in your room?","options":["Below €10.3M","€10.3M to €10.8M","€10.8M to €11.2M","Above €11.2M, or no deal"]},{"id":"s4-l3","q":"Which clause does your entity most often weaken or lose in negotiation?","options":["Audit, access and inspection","Exit strategy and transition period","Subcontracting conditions","Service and data locations","Termination rights and notice","None: we hold them all"]},{"id":"s4-l4","q":"When is the renewal of your biggest contract decided today?","options":["More than 12 months before expiry","9 to 12 months before","Less than 9 months before","After the notice deadline, or it auto-renews","I do not know"]}]}